🧩 Problem: Merge Two Sorted Lists

Given two sorted linked lists, merge them into one sorted list.

🧠 1. How to START in an interview (Brute force thinking)

You don’t jump to optimal immediately. Start simple:

💬 What you say

“Since both lists are sorted, one brute force way is to collect all elements, sort them, and rebuild the list.”

💡 Approach
Traverse both lists → put values in array
Sort array
Create new linked list
⏱ Complexity
Time: O((n+m) log(n+m))
Space: O(n+m)
❌ Why it's not ideal

“We’re not using the sorted nature efficiently, and we’re using extra space.”

🚀 2. Optimization Insight (Key jump)
💬 What you say

“Since both lists are already sorted, I can merge them like we do in merge sort.”

This is the critical transition interviewers look for.

⚙️ 3. Optimal Approach (Two pointers)
💡 Idea
Compare heads of both lists
Pick smaller node
Move that list forward
Repeat
🧠 Clean Explanation Flow (what you should say)

“I’ll maintain two pointers, one for each list.
At each step, I compare values and attach the smaller node to the result.
Then I move that pointer forward.
Once one list finishes, I attach the remaining part of the other list.”

✨ Implementation Styles
✅ Your approach (no dummy node)
Slightly more edge-case handling
Good, but interviewers prefer cleaner version
⭐ Preferred: Dummy Node Approach
ListNode dummy = new ListNode(-1);
ListNode tail = dummy;

while (list1 != null && list2 != null) {
    if (list1.val <= list2.val) {
        tail.next = list1;
        list1 = list1.next;
    } else {
        tail.next = list2;
        list2 = list2.next;
    }
    tail = tail.next;
}

tail.next = (list1 != null) ? list1 : list2;

return dummy.next;
⏱ Complexity
Time: O(n + m)
Space: O(1) (in-place)
👀 4. Visualization (VERY IMPORTANT for revision)
Example:
list1: 1 → 3 → 5
list2: 2 → 4 → 6
Step-by-step:
Compare 1 & 2 → pick 1
Result: 1

Compare 3 & 2 → pick 2
Result: 1 → 2

Compare 3 & 4 → pick 3
Result: 1 → 2 → 3

Compare 5 & 4 → pick 4
Result: 1 → 2 → 3 → 4

Compare 5 & 6 → pick 5
Result: 1 → 2 → 3 → 4 → 5

Attach remaining → 6
Final: 1 → 2 → 3 → 4 → 5 → 6
🔁 5. Mental Model (for quick recall)

When revising, just remember:

“Like merging two sorted arrays, but pointer manipulation instead of indices.”

OR even shorter:

“Pick smaller, move pointer, attach, repeat.”

🧱 6. Common Mistakes (you already hit one)
Updating wrong pointer (list1 = list2.next)
Forgetting to attach remaining list
Not moving tail
🎯 7. How to SOUND like SDE-2

Instead of just coding, say:

“This resembles merge step of merge sort”
“We optimize space by reusing existing nodes”
“We maintain stable ordering”
“Time complexity reduces to linear”
📝 If you’re putting this in notes (keep it tight)

You can summarize like:

Brute:
- Store + sort → O(n log n), extra space

Optimal:
- Two pointers
- Compare, attach smaller
- Move pointer
- Attach remaining

Time: O(n+m)
Space: O(1)

